 "function": "cloudgen.texture.regime_texture",
 "doc": "Plans in call order: 0-1 the generic fBm (seed+3, directions 0.75 p + 0.25 back), 2-3 the street fBm (seed+5, 0.1 p + 0.9 back), then a (worley3, cell_edges) pair for each USED cell diameter (seed + int(d_km), directions 0.85 p + 0.15 back); params.cells_used_km lists them, the fBm ladder is octave_ladder(fine_frequency, fmax) (the plans hold its result). The cells are accumulated in float32 and standardized over the WHOLE array (a global mean and std: the case is one array). The resolution cutoff on a 24x12 grid of float32 directions with float32 hand-built weights: radius 1200 km, fmax 10: f = 1200 / 120 = 10.0 exactly, so `f > fmax` is false and the 120 km cells ARE used (a `>=` would skip them: then only the two fBm fields remain). A port must compute f by a DIVISION, as numpy does (a multiplication by the reciprocal of 120 may land one ulp off and break the tie). The fBm ladder is octave_ladder(2, 10) (3 octaves up to 9.5 cycles per radian), gain 0.6. Measured worst difference of the texture 3.6e-5 against an all-float64 evaluation of the same plans and 2.6e-5 under one-ulp nudges: atol 4e-4, rtol 1e-5 (10x). Outputs besides `texture` are the intermediates, there to localize a mismatch: generic_std and street_std (the standardized fBm fields), and, when a cell diameter is used, closed_sum and open_sum (the float32 weighted sums of the cell ramps, before standardization) and closed_std / open_std (their standardizations); each has its own measured tolerance, see TEX_INTER in the case module. params.constants holds the mixes, ramps and seed offsets written in the code, and params.cells_per_radian the f = radius_km / d_km compared with fmax for each diameter (`f > fmax` skips, a tie f == fmax is USED; f is computed by a division).",
